 "notes": "160 junctions read off the 1912 City Topographic Survey (UTM 16N). Each point is where the two centrelines meet. Street edges on this sheet are drawn as double lines (curb plus lot line), so centres were taken between the inner lines, using measured ink profiles plus a visual check.\nNames are as printed: COLERAINE (not Colerain), BAYMILLER, AILANTHUS, WILMINK, KUKILL, ZINCK, KOEBEL, KIMPEL, ANDRESS, DECKEBACH, DIXMYTH, WIRHAM. PORTER and GIBSON are printed with no suffix.\nHarrison Avenue / Central Avenue: west of the Brighton corner (Coleraine x Harrison) the road is labelled HARRISON AVE. East of the corner, the tracked road past Kindel and Freeman carries no label inside the box. It continues ESE to the canal and is printed CENTRAL south of there, so it is recorded as Central Avenue (Kindel x Central, Freeman x Central).\nDivision Street (south of Harrison) and Spring Grove Avenue (north of Harrison) meet Harrison at one crossing, recorded as Spring Grove x Harrison.\nWestern Avenue meets Dayton Street twice, because Dayton is offset across Western; both T-junctions are recorded. The same goes for Buck Street at Harrison (offset legs, two T's), Coleraine x Hopple (offset, two T's), Coleraine x Bates (two T's) and Garrard x Hopple (south and north legs are offset by about 11 m).\nThere are two Kottman Streets west of McMicken, both printed KOTTMAN ST.: a lower one from Halstead to McMicken and an upper one west of Halstead.\nFreeman Avenue: Corn Alley (east side) and Garden Alley (west side) are offset by about 8 m, so they are recorded as two T's.\nGarrard x Township is an L at the south bend (Garrard turns into Township) plus a T at the west leg.\nSkipped: unnamed connectors, the McMicken fork near the canal (the branch from Coleraine is unlabelled), the INCLINE (inclined-plane railway), Atlas and Conroy Alleys (they meet no named street), the unnamed short street between Patterson and Coleraine just south of Harrison, and dashed paths.\nThe Mill Creek valley west of Spring Grove Avenue (rail yards and ponds) has no streets."
